1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,455
The same random forest scored 0.23 or 0.68,

2
00:00:04,455 --> 00:00:08,131
depending only on how the data was split.

3
00:00:09,631 --> 00:00:14,729
This project tests classical computer vision on 245 fabric strips.

4
00:00:15,179 --> 00:00:17,703
Strips are cut into overlapping patches.

5
00:00:17,703 --> 00:00:21,046
Split by patch, and test texture leaks into training.

6
00:00:21,496 --> 00:00:24,824
So I split by whole strip, and held out entire fabrics.

7
00:00:26,324 --> 00:00:29,121
Each patch gets ninety texture features.

8
00:00:29,121 --> 00:00:31,359
Supervised models learn defects.

9
00:00:31,359 --> 00:00:34,367
Anomaly detectors learn only normal fabric.

10
00:00:34,817 --> 00:00:38,955
Everything is scored three ways, including a deliberately leaky control.

11
00:00:40,455 --> 00:00:42,311
This is the results explorer.

12
00:00:42,761 --> 00:00:47,386
For the random forest, patch precision is 0.23

13
00:00:47,386 --> 00:00:51,809
split by strip, and 0.68 with leaky patches.

14
00:00:51,809 --> 00:00:57,439
A paired difference of plus 0.44, interval 0.27 to 0.53.

15
00:00:57,889 --> 00:01:02,578
On an unseen fabric, nothing clearly beats chance.

16
00:01:02,578 --> 00:01:08,299
The best of eight methods scores 0.59, interval 0.44 to 0.72.

17
00:01:08,749 --> 00:01:10,349
Every strip can be inspected.

18
00:01:10,349 --> 00:01:11,949
Here are the forest's misses.

19
00:01:12,399 --> 00:01:15,345
Some results look good for the wrong reasons.

20
00:01:15,345 --> 00:01:18,487
Brightness alone reaches 0.83 within one fabric.

21
00:01:18,487 --> 00:01:21,039
Those strips were captured differently.

22
00:01:22,539 --> 00:01:25,294
The limits are real: one small dataset,

23
00:01:25,294 --> 00:01:28,897
7 fabrics, precomputed results, no factory testing.

24
00:01:29,347 --> 00:01:34,125
204 of 245 strips also have blank margins.

25
00:01:35,625 --> 00:01:38,356
Code, tables and the explorer are all open.
